[A,README.md,1] #fastecdsa
[A,README.md,2] 
[A,README.md,3] **DO NOT USE THIS PACKAGE FOR PROD, I HAVE NOT MITIGATED SIDE CHANNELS YET**
[A,README.md,4] 
[A,README.md,5] This is a python package for doing elliptic curve cryptography, specifically
[A,README.md,6] digital signatures. Currently it does basic point multiplication significantly
[A,README.md,7] faster than the `ecdsa` package. You can see the times for 10,000 point
[A,README.md,8] multiplications below, `fast.py` corresponding to this package and `regular.py`
[A,README.md,9] corresponding to `ecdsa` package.
[A,README.md,10] 
[A,README.md,11] ![Performance](http://i.imgur.com/olqitSs.png?1)
[A,README.md,12] 
[A,README.md,13] As you can see, this package in this case is ~50x faster.
[A,README.md,14] 
[A,README.md,15] # Supported Curves
[A,README.md,16] * P256
[A,README.md,17] 
[A,README.md,18] #Installing
[A,README.md,19] Run `python setup.py install`. Note that you need to have C compiler that
[A,README.md,20] supports the C99  standard. You also need to have [GMP](https://gmplib.org/) on
[A,README.md,21] your system as the underlying C code in this package includes the `gmp.h` header
[A,README.md,22] (and links against gmp via the `-lgmp` flag).
[A,README.md,23] 
[A,README.md,24] #Usage
[A,README.md,25] Some kinks are still being worked out, so far limited usage is as follows:
[A,README.md,26] 
[A,README.md,27] ```python
[A,README.md,28] import fastecdsa
[A,README.md,29] 
[A,README.md,30] x, y = (..., ...)  # whatever curve coords you want
[A,README.md,31] d = ...  # some scalar
[A,README.md,32] rx, ry = fastecdsa.mul(map(str, (x, y, d)))
[A,README.md,33] ```
[A,README.md,34] 
[A,README.md,35] Note that `rx` and `ry` are returned as strings. You can cast them to ints if
[A,README.md,36] need be e.g. `map(int, (rx, ry))`.
